    expectedAnswer: "A binary search tree (BST) maintains an ordering rule: values in the left subtree are smaller than the node and values in the right subtree are larger, subject to the problem's duplicate policy. Search, insert, and delete are O(h), where h is tree height.",
    deepExplanation: "Step 1 — BST node:\n```ts\ninterface TreeNode {\n  value: number;\n  left: TreeNode | null;\n  right: TreeNode | null;\n}\n```\n\nWITHOUT BUILT-IN / CORE search:\n```ts\ninterface TreeNode {\n  value: number;\n  left: TreeNode | null;\n  right: TreeNode | null;\n}\n\nfunction searchBST(\n  root: TreeNode | null,\n  target: number,\n): TreeNode | null {\n  let current = root;\n\n  while (current !== null) {\n    if (current.value === target) return current;\n\n    if (target < current.value) {\n      current = current.left;\n    } else {\n      current = current.right;\n    }\n  }\n\n  return null;\n}\n```\n\nWITH BUILT-IN-friendly inorder validation:\n```ts\ninterface TreeNode {\n  value: number;\n  left: TreeNode | null;\n  right: TreeNode | null;\n}\n\nfunction isValidBST(root: TreeNode | null): boolean {\n  const values: number[] = [];\n\n  function inorder(node: TreeNode | null): void {\n    if (node === null) return;\n    inorder(node.left);\n    values.push(node.value);\n    inorder(node.right);\n  }\n\n  inorder(root);\n\n  for (let i = 1; i < values.length; i++) {\n    if (values[i - 1]! >= values[i]!) return false;\n  }\n\n  return true;\n}\n```\n\nStep 2 — Example:\n```text\n      8\n     /     3   10\n   / \\      1   6    14\n```\nSearch 6:\n8 → left\n3 → right\n6 → found.\n\nStep 3 — Complexity:\nSearch O(h).\nBalanced BST → O(log n).\nWorst-case skewed BST → O(n).\n\nStep 4 — Interview rule:\nAlways state the tree-height assumption.",
