    title: "Lowest common ancestor",
    difficulty: "Medium",
    experienceLevel: "2–5 Years",
    category: "Trees",
    expectedAnswer: "For a general binary tree, the lowest common ancestor (LCA) is the deepest node that has both target nodes in its subtree. A postorder-style DFS can return whether each subtree contains either target and identify the split point.",
    deepExplanation: "Step 1 — General binary tree solution:\n\n```ts\ninterface TreeNode {\n  value: number;\n  left: TreeNode | null;\n  right: TreeNode | null;\n}\n```\n\nWITHOUT BUILT-IN / CORE:\n```ts\ninterface TreeNode {\n  value: number;\n  left: TreeNode | null;\n  right: TreeNode | null;\n}\n\nfunction lowestCommonAncestor(\n  root: TreeNode | null,\n  first: TreeNode,\n  second: TreeNode,\n): TreeNode | null {\n  if (root === null || root === first || root === second) {\n    return root;\n  }\n\n  const left = lowestCommonAncestor(root.left, first, second);\n  const right = lowestCommonAncestor(root.right, first, second);\n\n  if (left !== null && right !== null) return root;\n\n  return left ?? right;\n}\n```\n\nWITH BUILT-IN-friendly parent-map approach:\n```ts\ninterface TreeNode {\n  value: number;\n  left: TreeNode | null;\n  right: TreeNode | null;\n}\n\nfunction lowestCommonAncestorUsingParentMap(\n  root: TreeNode | null,\n  first: TreeNode,\n  second: TreeNode,\n): TreeNode | null {\n  if (root === null) return null;\n\n  const parent = new Map<TreeNode, TreeNode | null>();\n  const stack: TreeNode[] = [root];\n  parent.set(root, null);\n\n  while (stack.length > 0) {\n    const node = stack.pop()!;\n\n    if (node.left !== null && !parent.has(node.left)) {\n      parent.set(node.left, node);\n      stack.push(node.left);\n    }\n\n    if (node.right !== null && !parent.has(node.right)) {\n      parent.set(node.right, node);\n      stack.push(node.right);\n    }\n  }\n\n  const ancestors = new Set<TreeNode>();\n  let current: TreeNode | null = first;\n\n  while (current !== null) {\n    ancestors.add(current);\n    current = parent.get(current) ?? null;\n  }\n\n  current = second;\n\n  while (current !== null) {\n    if (ancestors.has(current)) return current;\n    current = parent.get(current) ?? null;\n  }\n\n  return null;\n}\n```\n\nStep 2 — Example:\n```text\n      3\n     /     5   1\n   /   6   2\n```\nLCA(6,2) = 5.\n\nStep 3 — Complexity:\nRecursive DFS O(n) time, O(h) stack.\nParent-map method O(n) time, O(n) extra space.\n\nStep 4 — Note:\nFor a BST, a different O(h) value-based approach is available.",
